<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Evaluate the Definite integral by partial fraction</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>1. Write the expression below as a sum of its partial fractions.</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle {@question_expression@}=\) [[input:ans1]]+[[input:ans2]]+[[input:ans3]] <br></p>
<p>[[validation:ans1]][[validation:ans2]][[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>2. Using the partial fractions above to compute the integral&nbsp;\( \displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{@upper_limit@} {@question_expression@}dx\).&nbsp;<br><br><br></p>
<p>\( \displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{@upper_limit@} {@question_expression@}dx =\) [[input:ans4]][[validation:ans4]][[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><span><strong>Worked solution</strong></span></p>
<p>In this case the denominator is already factored and so we go straight to the form of the partial fraction for the integrand.</p>

<p style=""> To write \( \displaystyle {@question_expression@}\) as a sum of its partial fractions we need to follow the steps below:</p>
<p style="">\[{@question_expression@} ={@part_frac_A@}+{@part_frac_B@}+{@part_frac_C@}\]</p>
<p style="">Setting the numerators equal gives:</p>
<p style="">\[{@expr@}\]</p>
<p style="">Getting the coefficients A, B and C we have</p>
<p style="">Let \(x={@-bb@}:\quad {@expr1@} \implies {@expB@}\). </p>
<p style="">Let \(x={@-aa@}:\quad {@expr2@} \implies {@expA@}\).</p>
<p style="">Let \(x={@-cc@}:\quad {@expr3@} \implies {@expC@}\). </p>
<p style="">The partial fraction of the integrand is then,</p>
<p style="">\[{@expr4@}\]</p>
<p style="">Evaluating the integral,</p>


<p><span style="font-size: 0.9375rem;">\[\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{@upper_limit@} {@question_expression@}dx= \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{@upper_limit@} {@part_frac_1@}\,dx+&nbsp;\int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{@upper_limit@}{@part_frac_2@}\,dx+&nbsp;\int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{@upper_limit@}{@part_frac_3@}dx\]</span><br></p>

<p></p>\[\iff \displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{@upper_limit@} {@question_expression@}dx={@int_ta1@}+{@int_ta2@}+{@int_ta3@}\]<br>Therefore,<br>\[\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{@upper_limit@} {@question_expression@}dx={@ta@}\]<br><p></p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
